  VII. THE IDENTITY REFORMULATED
  --------------------------------------------------

  sigma(n) - 2n = phi(n)
  <=> [sum of proper divisors] - n = phi(n)
  <=> s(n) - n = phi(n)  where s(n) = sigma(n) - n (aliquot sum)
  <=> abundance(n) = phi(n)

  For n=42: s(42) = sigma(42)-42 = 54
  abundance(42) = s(42)-42 = 12
  phi(42) = 12
  s(42) - 42 = 12 = phi(42)? True

  42 is ABUNDANT: s(42) = 54 > 42.
  Its abundance = 12 = phi(42) = 12.
  The excess divisor weight EXACTLY equals the coprime count.

  VIII. B_42 AND THE SYLVESTER CONNECTION
  --------------------------------------------------

  B_42 =  1520097643918070802691/1806
  Numerator:   1520097643918070802691
  Denominator: 1806

  Factor denom(B_42) = 1806:
  1806 = 2 * 3 * 7 * 43

  Von Staudt-Clausen: denom(B_{42}) = prod{p prime : (p-1)|42}
  Divisors of 42: [1, 2, 3, 6, 7, 14, 21, 42]
  p-1 in divisors of 42, so p in: [2, 3, 7, 43]
  Product: 1
  Product = 2*3*7*43 = 1806
  Matches denom(B_42)? True

  KEY: 43 is in the denominator because (43-1)=42 divides 42!
  43 is the Sylvester successor: 42+1 = 43.
  The Bernoulli number B_42 'knows' about the next integer after 42.

  IX. THE CHAIN: B_6 -> 42 -> B_42 -> 43
  --------------------------------------------------

  B_6 = 1/42:    denom involves {2, 3, 7}, product = 42
  B_42 = N/D:     denom involves primes p with (p-1)|42
  These primes: [2, 3, 7, 43]
  43 IS PRIME, and 42 | 42, so 43 is in denom(B_42).

  The Sylvester sequence: 2, 3, 7, 43, 1807, 3263443, ...
  Each term n_k: (p-1) | n_{k-1} * ... * n_1, so p = n_k + 1
  appears in B_{n_{k-1} * ... * n_1}.

  43 is prime: True
  1807 = 42*43+1 = 42*43+1. Prime? NO! 1807 = 13 * 139

  Sylvester's sequence (a_1=2, a_{n+1} = prod(a_1..a_n) + 1):
    a_2 = 3
    a_3 = 7
    a_4 = 43
    a_5 = 1807
    a_6 = 3263443
    a_7 = 10650056950807

  Sylvester sequence: [2, 3, 7, 43, 1807]
  Our chain (from B_6): 2, 3, 7, 43, ...
  Match through first 4 terms!

